Relevant columns and their potential usage  

• “block_timestamp”: extract year/month information and calculate the number of seconds in each month.  
• “transaction_hash”: lets you count how many transaction‑records (rows) exist, even if the same hash appears many times (no dedup needed).  
• “block_number”, “block_hash”, “log_index”, “transaction_index”: not required for the asked KPI, but helpful for sanity‑checks.  
• Filter condition that will be useful:  
  – Only rows whose “block_timestamp” falls inside calendar year 2023.  
  – Convert micro‑second epoch (“block_timestamp”) into TIMESTAMP to slice by month.  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL statements (simple → complex) that can be run to understand the data and finally arrive at the requested result set.  
All column names are enclosed in double quotation marks and every query is limited to 20 rows unless an aggregate is being produced.

```sql
--Description: Peek at a few raw log rows to understand available fields.
SELECT *
FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Show 20 sample values of block_timestamp converted to human‑readable time.
SELECT "block_timestamp",
       TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000) AS "readable_ts"
FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: List the distinct calendar years present in the table.
SELECT DISTINCT EXTRACT(year FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) AS "year"
FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
ORDER BY "year";
```

```sql
--Description: Verify that rows exist for every month of 2023.
SELECT DISTINCT EXTRACT(month FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) AS "month"
FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
WHERE EXTRACT(year FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) = 2023
ORDER BY "month";
```

```sql
--Description: Count total log‑rows (transaction records) inside 2023.
SELECT COUNT(*) AS "total_rows_2023"
FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
WHERE EXTRACT(year FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) = 2023;
```

```sql
--Description: Show raw monthly counts for 2023 (without yet computing TPS).
SELECT EXTRACT(year  FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) AS "year",
       EXTRACT(month FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) AS "month",
       COUNT(*) AS "monthly_txn_rows"
FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
WHERE EXTRACT(year FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) = 2023
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY "monthly_txn_rows" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Compute the exact number of seconds in each 2023 month (leap‑year logic is automatic).
WITH months AS (
  SELECT
      DATE_TRUNC(month, TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000))            AS "month_start",
      DATEADD(month, 1, DATE_TRUNC(month, TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000))) AS "month_end"
  FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
  WHERE EXTRACT(year FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) = 2023
  GROUP BY 1
)
SELECT "month_start",
       DATEDIFF(second, "month_start", "month_end") AS "seconds_in_month"
FROM months
ORDER BY "month_start"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Join the seconds‑per‑month back to the counts to compute TPS for each month of 2023.
WITH base AS (
  SELECT DATE_TRUNC(month, TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) AS "month_start",
         COUNT(*)                                                     AS "monthly_txn_rows"
  FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
  WHERE EXTRACT(year FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) = 2023
  GROUP BY 1
),
seconds AS (
  SELECT "month_start",
         DATEDIFF(second,
                  "month_start",
                  DATEADD(month, 1, "month_start"))                  AS "seconds_in_month"
  FROM base
)
SELECT b."month_start",
       b."monthly_txn_rows",
       ROUND(b."monthly_txn_rows" / s."seconds_in_month", 6)         AS "txns_per_second"
FROM base b
JOIN seconds s USING ("month_start")
ORDER BY b."monthly_txn_rows" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Do the same computation but pull data from the DECODED_EVENTS table instead of LOGS.
WITH base AS (
  SELECT DATE_TRUNC(month, TO_TIMESTAMP("BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" / 1000000)) AS "month_start",
         COUNT(*)                                                     AS "monthly_event_rows"
  FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."DECODED_EVENTS"
  WHERE EXTRACT(year FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" / 1000000)) = 2023
  GROUP BY 1
),
seconds AS (
  SELECT "month_start",
         DATEDIFF(second,
                  "month_start",
                  DATEADD(month, 1, "month_start"))                  AS "seconds_in_month"
  FROM base
)
SELECT b."month_start",
       b."monthly_event_rows",
       ROUND(b."monthly_event_rows" / s."seconds_in_month", 6)        AS "events_per_second"
FROM base b
JOIN seconds s USING ("month_start")
ORDER BY b."monthly_event_rows" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: (Final) Produce the requested result set – monthly counts and TPS for 2023, sorted by descending count.
WITH monthly AS (
  SELECT
      EXTRACT(year  FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000))  AS "year",
      EXTRACT(month FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000))  AS "month",
      COUNT(*)                                                       AS "monthly_txn_rows"
  FROM "GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN"."GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US"."LOGS"
  WHERE EXTRACT(year FROM TO_TIMESTAMP("block_timestamp" / 1000000)) = 2023
  GROUP BY 1, 2
),
seconds AS (
  SELECT
      "year",
      "month",
      DATEDIFF(
          second,
          TO_DATE(TO_VARCHAR("year") || '-' || TO_VARCHAR("month") || '-01'),
          DATEADD(month, 1, TO_DATE(TO_VARCHAR("year") || '-' || TO_VARCHAR("month") || '-01'))
      ) AS "seconds_in_month"
  FROM monthly
)
SELECT
    m."year",
    m."month",
    m."monthly_txn_rows",
    ROUND(m."monthly_txn_rows" / s."seconds_in_month", 6) AS "txns_per_second"
FROM monthly m
JOIN seconds s USING ("year", "month")
ORDER BY m."monthly_txn_rows" DESC;
```

